  "summary": "Four candidates directly apply multilevel probabilistic models with partial pooling (i.e., Bayesian hierarchical models) to predicting protein molecular function. [V0164] (Fischer et al., 2022) applies the method on MetroFlow with a test F1 of 0.87. [V0163] (El-Sayed et al., 2021) applies it on ChurnBench, reporting AUROC 0.8. [V0162] (Duarte et al., 2020) applies it on FireScope, obtaining MAE 0.73. [V0161] (Chen et al., 2019) applies it on ProtMap with accuracy 0.66. Metrics are not directly comparable across these studies (F1, AUROC, MAE, accuracy), so ordering reflects reported performance within each evaluation setup. The fifth slot goes to [V0155], which addresses the same topic (predicting protein molecular function, accuracy 0.93 on ChurnBench) but uses decentralised training rather than the requested method; it is included only as the nearest topical comparator, not as a direct application. Candidates such as [V0064], [V0055], [V0034], and [V0221] merely mention the method-topic pair as prior work and are not applications; other protein-function studies ([V0165], [V0159], [V0147], [V0151], [V0146]) use different methods entirely.",
